  "ivalue_usage": {
    "description": "Phase 1: ten ways of USING an I-value, against the i.i.d. sampler that beats all of them so far. Selecting by predicted I-value has lost three independent comparisons, and Phase 0 found the likely reason: an i-value walk's batches are 2.3x less diverse than i.i.d. ones (mean pairwise embedding distance 0.0385 vs 0.0872, p10 0.0121 vs 0.0623), because its argmax ranges over the current node's k-NN neighbours -- similar faces by construction. So treat_pool_only is the headline arm: it keeps the estimator and the selection rule and changes only the locality. The rest test mechanisms that are not 'pick the argmax': weighting instead of selecting (keeps i.i.d. coverage), banning nodes whose measured gain stays negative (38% of trained samples show one), targeting weak demographic GROUPS but sampling uniformly inside them (group means cancel per-sample label noise), a mid-quantile band instead of the extreme (treat_band_mid_pool: a hard cutoff), and its smooth analogue (treat_midband_*: every candidate stays eligible with a probability/weight that peaks on a mid-high plateau and tapers -- never zero -- toward both the low end, already mastered, and the high end, where mislabels and corrupted samples land on this 87.55%-imbalanced corpus, rather than hard-excluding either). Every arm trains on exactly 10,000 nodes/epoch for 15 epochs and records its own selection diagnostic, so what each arm trained on is measured rather than reconstructed afterwards.",
